.Dd August 10, 2026
.Dt SLOWHTTPTEST-NG 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm slowhttptest-ng
.Nd measure whether a server keeps serving under slow HTTP load
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Bk -words
.Op Fl H | B | R | X
.Op Fl u Ar url
.Op Fl c Ar connections
.Op Fl r Ar rate
.Op Fl l Ar seconds
.Op Fl i Ar seconds
.Op Fl g
.Op Fl o Ar base
.Op Fl p Ar seconds
.Op Fl d Ar host : Ns Ar port
.Op Fl e Ar host : Ns Ar port
.Op Fl 1 Ar header
.Op Fl P Ar data
.Op Fl A Ar user-agent
.Op Fl q
.Op options ...
.Ek
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
tests whether a web server remains available to ordinary clients while it is
subjected to slow HTTP load.
It opens many connections that make progress
extremely slowly, and, independently, keeps one ordinary client of its own
alive to measure whether the service is still being served.
.Pp
That second part is the point.
Held sockets prove nothing on their own: an
event-driven server will hold every connection you can open and serve every
real client throughout.
The verdict is derived from the availability probe, not
from socket counts.
.Pp
Only run
.Nm
against systems you are authorized to test.
.Ss Test modes
Each mode attacks a different point of the request lifecycle, and each is
stopped by a different server timeout.
A server hardened against one can be
wide open to the next.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl H
Slow headers, a.k.a. Slowloris.
Sends a request whose headers never end.
The
server blocks reading headers; the defense is a request-header timeout.
This is
the default.
.It Fl B
Slow body, a.k.a. R-U-Dead-Yet.
Sends complete headers with a large
.Sy Content-Length ,
then delivers the body a few bytes at a time.
The server blocks reading the
body.
Because the headers completed instantly, a header timeout never fires;
the defense is a request-body timeout.
.It Fl R
Range attack, a.k.a. Apache killer.
A small request naming thousands of
overlapping byte ranges, which a vulnerable server expands in memory.
CVE-2011-3192 was patched in 2011, so against a current server this is a
regression check rather than a live exploit.
.It Fl X
Slow read.
Sends a complete, valid request and then refuses to read the
response, advertising a tiny TCP receive window.
The server blocks
.Em sending .
Neither a header nor a body timeout helps; the defense is a send timeout.
.El
.Ss General options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl u Ar url
Absolute URL of the target,
.Sy http
or
.Sy https .
Default
.Pa http://localhost/ .
.It Fl c Ar connections
Target number of concurrent connections.
Default 50.
.It Fl r Ar rate
New connections per second.
Default 50.
.It Fl -connect-timeout Ar seconds
Drop a connection that has not been established within this long and reuse the
slot.
Default 10; 0 leaves it to the operating system.
.Pp
The OS default is far too patient for this purpose \(em macOS retries a SYN for
75 seconds.
Against a target that drops SYNs, a rate limiter or a full accept queue, every
slot fills with connections that will never establish, the attack quietly stops
applying pressure, and the status line shows a large
.Dq connecting
count that never resolves.
Recycling the slot sends a fresh SYN instead.
.It Fl -max-connecting Ar n
Ceiling on connections simultaneously mid-handshake.
Default 5000; 0 removes the limit.
.Pp
This is not here to stop you flooding a slow-accepting target \(em exhausting an
accept queue is a real attack and worth testing.
It is here because the cost of holding half-open sockets has a knee in it.
Time for the operating system to reap the process on Ctrl-C, measured on macOS
against a target answering no SYNs at all:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
 2500 in flight   0.0 s     7000 in flight    7.4 s
 5000 in flight   0.6 s     9000 in flight   14.2 s
 6000 in flight   3.1 s    10000 in flight   23.5 s
.Ed
.Pp
The default sits at that knee, so the ordinary case pays nothing and a larger
flood is asked for explicitly with
.Fl -max-connecting Cm 0 .
Against a target that accepts normally the cap never binds: handshakes complete
in milliseconds, so even a fast ramp keeps only a few hundred in flight.
.It Fl l Ar seconds
Test length.
Default 240.
.It Fl i Ar seconds
Interval between followup data.
Default 10.
.It Fl x Ar bytes
Maximum length of each randomized name/value pair of followup data.
Default 32.
.It Fl s Ar bytes
Value of the
.Sy Content-Length
header for body modes.
Default 4096.
.It Fl t Ar verb
Request verb.
Defaults to GET for slow headers and slow read, POST for slow
body, HEAD for range.
.It Fl f Ar content-type
Value of the
.Sy Content-Type
header.
.It Fl m Ar accept
Value of the
.Sy Accept
header.
.It Fl j Ar cookie
Value of the
.Sy Cookie
header.
.It Fl 1 Ar header , Fl -header Ar header
Extra header, e.g.
.Dq Li "Authorization: Bearer abc" .
May be given more than once.
Sent on every attack request
.Em and
on the availability probe, so both reach the same endpoint.
Values containing
CR or LF are rejected.
.It Fl P Ar data , Fl -data Ar data
Request body for
.Fl B .
A leading
.Sq @
reads the body from a file, as in
.Xr curl 1 .
.Fl s
is raised above the payload if needed, so the body still never completes.
.It Fl A Ar user-agent , Fl -user-agent Ar user-agent
Value of the
.Sy User-Agent
header.
By default
.Nm
identifies itself and carries the project URL, so the traffic is recognisable
in the target's own logs.
.It Fl -random-user-agent
Present as Chrome, Firefox or Safari instead, one chosen per run.
Use when a
CDN or WAF sheds the tool's own agent before it reaches the service.
The agent
used is recorded in the report.
.It Fl -no-referer
Do not send the
.Sy Referer
header.
.Nm
sends
.Dq Li TESTING_PURPOSES_ONLY
by default as a second marker for defenders.
Drop it if the target rejects an
unparseable Referer, or replace it with
.Fl 1 Dq Li "Referer: https://..." .
.It Fl v Ar level
Verbosity, 0 to 4, on the classic scale: fatal, info, error, warn, debug.
Default 1.
.Bl -tag -width 4n -compact
.It 0
Silent, the same as
.Fl q .
.It 1
The banner, the running status and the verdict.
.It 2
Adds every connection error as it happens, rather than the first one and a
tally at the end.
.It 3
Adds connection-level warnings: a handshake stalled past
.Fl \-connect-timeout
and its slot reclaimed, a peer that hung up on a connection that is therefore
holding nothing.
.It 4
Adds per-socket tracing: descriptor numbers, where each connected, bytes
written, and each close.
Also a breakdown of connect failures by cause, which matters when several are
mixed and the dominant one alone would mislead.
.El
.Pp
At 2 and above the running status stops being redrawn in place and becomes one
appended line per update.
The two cannot share a terminal: the status block repaints over its own lines,
so a per-connection trace printed into it would be overwritten a moment later.
Verbose output is a log, not a dashboard.
.It Fl q , Fl -quiet
No console output at all.
Reports are still written and the exit status is
unchanged.
Equivalent to
.Fl v Cm 0 .
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version, project URL and whether TLS is compiled in, then exit.
.El
.Ss Proxy options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d Ar host : Ns Ar port
Route all traffic through this HTTP proxy.
.Sy https
targets are tunnelled with CONNECT;
.Sy http
requests use absolute-URI form.
.It Fl e Ar host : Ns Ar port
Route only the availability probe through this proxy, to measure availability
from a different network path than the one being saturated.
.It Fl \-http2
Speak HTTP/2 instead of HTTP/1.1.
Implemented for slow read
.Pq Fl X
only; the other modes rest on HTTP/1.1 framing with no direct equivalent.
.Pp
HTTP/2 makes slow read strictly stronger.
It adds an application-layer flow-control window that the kernel does not clamp,
so this opens that window to its maximum while starving the TCP window
underneath: the server generates the response, cannot hand it over, and holds it
in its own buffers rather than in socket buffers.
That is CVE-2019-9517, internal data buffering.
Multiplexing also pins many streams inside one connection, so per-IP connection
limits, the usual Slowloris mitigation, do not see it.
.It Fl \-h2-streams Ar n
Streams to pin per connection with
.Fl \-http2 .
Default 100.
.It Fl \-rapid-reset
HTTP/2 rapid reset, CVE-2023-44487.
Implies
.Fl \-http2 .
.Pp
Opens a stream and cancels it immediately, repeatedly.
A cancelled stream stops counting against
.Dv SETTINGS_MAX_CONCURRENT_STREAMS
the moment it is reset, but the server has already accepted the headers, routed
the request and often dispatched it, so the concurrency limit never binds while
the work does.
.Pp
The mitigation every implementation shipped is to count resets and close the
connection once there are too many, so a server that has been fixed answers by
hanging up.
That arrives here as a peer close and is reported as such; a target that lets
this run indefinitely has not been fixed.
.It Fl \-continuation-flood
HTTP/2 CONTINUATION flood.
Implies
.Fl \-http2 .
.Pp
The direct analogue of slow headers
.Pq Fl H ,
and worse than its ancestor in two ways.
A header block may be split across a
.Dv HEADERS
frame and any number of
.Dv CONTINUATION
frames, and is only complete when one carries
.Dv END_HEADERS .
Until then the server cannot act on it: HPACK is a stateful compression context,
so a partial block cannot be decoded, dispatched or discarded without
desynchronising every later block on that connection.
It has to be kept.
This opens a block and never closes it.
.Pp
So the cost is memory that grows for as long as the client keeps sending, rather
than one pinned connection slot.
And in many stacks all of this happens before the point where a request is
logged, so a server can be driven hard with nothing in its access log \-\- the
operator sees memory climb and no requests to explain it.
Observed against nginx 1.31.4, which closed every connection on its header
limit and wrote nothing at all to the error log at
.Cm warn
level.
.Pp
.Fl i
and
.Fl x
mean what they mean for slow headers: how often another fragment goes out, and
how large the random field inside it is.
.It Fl \-h2-reset-rate Ar n
Streams opened and cancelled per second, per connection, with
.Fl \-rapid-reset .
Default 100.
.Pp
Rate-limited deliberately.
This is a load generator aimed at a known amplifier, and at the default
connection count it already asks a vulnerable server to process several thousand
requests a second.
.It Fl 4 , Fl \-ipv4
.It Fl 6 , Fl \-ipv6
Resolve the target to IPv4 or IPv6 addresses only.
.Pp
This is about reproducibility rather than connectivity.
On a dual-stack host the resolver may answer with either family first, and it is
free to answer differently on the next invocation; the two families can be
entirely different network paths, with different routing, MTU and filtering.
A run that is not pinned can therefore land somewhere else than the previous one
and disagree with it for reasons nothing in the output explains.
Pin the family when comparing runs, and note that
.Nm
prints the address it actually used.
.El
.Ss Availability probe
The probe is what the verdict is based on.
One short-lived, entirely ordinary
request at a time, timing the first response byte.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl p Ar seconds
Probe timeout.
No response within this counts as unavailable.
Default 5.
.It Fl -probe-interval Ar seconds
Seconds between probes.
Accepts fractional values.
Default 2.
.It Fl -no-probe
Do not measure availability at all.
No verdict and no report are produced.
.El
.Pp
The status code deliberately does not decide availability: a
.Sy 503
is a served response, and treating it as a denial would conflate
.Dq it refused me
with
.Dq it never answered me .
See
.Fl -fail-on-status .
.Ss Capacity search
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -capacity
Hold connections at each level, probe, then step up, until the service stops
answering.
The denial threshold is reported as a bracket whose resolution is
the step size.
.It Fl -capacity-start Ar n
First level.
Default 32.
.It Fl -capacity-step Ar n
Increment between levels.
Default 32.
.It Fl -capacity-max Ar n
Ceiling.
Defaults to
.Fl c .
.It Fl -capacity-hold Ar seconds
Time held at each level.
Default 15.
.El
.Pp
Without
.Fl -capacity
no denial threshold is reported at all, and the report says why: at the default
rate the connection ramp covers far more connections than the probe samples, so
any number would be an artifact of ramp timing rather than a property of the
server.
.Ss Reporting
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl g
Write a report.
.It Fl o Ar base
Report base name; writes
.Pa base.html
and
.Pa base.json .
Default
.Pa slowhttptest-ng .
.It Fl -availability-threshold Ar fraction
Fraction of the attack window that must have been served for the JSON
criterion to pass.
Default 0.95.
.It Fl -fail-on-status Ar list
Status codes that additionally fail the CI criterion, e.g.
.Cm 5xx
or
.Dq Li 503,429 .
This affects the pass/fail gate only.
The measured outcome and the availability
figure are unchanged, because the server did answer.
.El
.Ss Range options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a Ar start
Left boundary of the ranges in the Range header.
Default 5.
.It Fl b Ar bytes
Ceiling for the Range header right boundary.
Default 2000.
.El
.Ss Slow read options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl n Ar seconds
Interval between read operations on the receive buffer.
Default 1.
.It Fl z Ar bytes
Bytes to read from the receive buffer per read call.
Default 5.
.It Fl w Ar bytes
Start of the range the advertised window size is picked from.
Default 1.
.It Fl y Ar bytes
End of that range.
Default 512.
.Pp
These set
.Dv SO_RCVBUF ,
which the kernel is free to override, and some do.
On macOS the receive buffer is resized when the connection establishes and
keeps growing afterwards, so a requested 200 bytes becomes several hundred
kilobytes and
.Fl w Ns / Ns Fl y
do not control the window at all.
The run still works, but each connection absorbs far more of the response than
asked before it backs up.
.Nm
detects this, warns, names the cause, and records it in the report as
.Li recv_buffer_overridden ,
because it changes what the run measured rather than only how it was logged.
Results from a platform that overrides are not comparable with one that does
not.
.It Fl k Ar n
Repeat the request this many times per connection, to multiply the response
size on servers supporting persistent connections.
Default 1.
.El
.Pp
Over
.Sy https ,
.Fl z
throttles how fast the application consumes plaintext while the kernel receive
buffer drains a TLS record at a time, because TLS is record-oriented.
The
attack still works; the effective granularity is a record rather than
.Fl z
bytes.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev SSL_CERT
Path to a PEM client certificate chain, for mutually authenticated
.Sy https
targets.
.It Ev SSL_KEY
Path to the matching PEM private key.
Both must be set together.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa base.html
Self-contained report.
All CSS, SVG and data are inlined, so it opens offline,
inside a container and on an air-gapped network.
.It Pa base.json
The same run in machine-readable form, from the same internal event log, so the
two cannot disagree.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
The test ran to completion.
This is returned regardless of the outcome:
finding a denial of service is a successful test run, not a failed one.
Gate
automation on
.Li .criterion.pass
in the JSON report instead.
.It 2
Usage or configuration error, or an
.Sy https
target on a build without TLS.
.It 3
No usable connection was ever established, so nothing was actually tested.
.It 130
Interrupted twice.
The second
.Dv SIGINT
exits immediately without writing anything.
.It 4
The event loop failed partway through.
No verdict and no report are produced, because measurements taken after the
loop stopped working are not trustworthy.
.El
.Ss Interrupting a run
.Dv SIGINT
(Ctrl-C) or
.Dv SIGTERM
quits at once.
Nothing is closed and nothing is written: no verdict, no report.
.Pp
A run that appears not to have exited has almost certainly exited already.
A finished process the shell has not yet reaped remains visible as a zombie,
which is easily mistaken for a hang: it is listed by
.Xr ps 1
with its command name in parentheses because its argument vector is gone, it
consumes no processor time,
.Dv SIGKILL
has no effect on it, and it vanishes the moment its parent shell does.
.Pp
To tell the two apart:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lsof -p <pid> | wc -l
.Ed
.Pp
No open descriptors means a zombie.
Sockets still listed by
.Xr netstat 1
at that point belong to the kernel's own teardown and to no process.
.Xr pgrep 1
with
.Fl f
will not match it either, there being no command line left to match.
.Ss Connection ceiling
Three reactor backends sit behind one interface:
.Sy kqueue
on macOS and the BSDs,
.Sy epoll
on Linux, and
.Sy poll
as the portable fallback.
.Nm Fl V
reports which a build will use and what ceiling it has, and every run logs the
one it actually used \(em they differ when
.Ev SLOWHTTP_REACTOR
is set, or when a sandbox refuses
.Xr epoll_create1 2
and the run falls back.
.Pp
The
.Sy poll
reactor cannot watch more descriptors than the platform allows, and on some
platforms that is a fixed
.Dv OPEN_MAX
rather than something the file descriptor limit controls \(em on Darwin it is
10240 whatever
.Ic ulimit Fl n
says.
.Sy kqueue
and
.Sy epoll
are bounded only by the descriptor limit.
.Pp
The backends also differ in cost, which matters at scale rather than for
correctness: holding 8000 connections costs about 7 times more CPU under
.Sy poll
than under
.Sy epoll .
A
.Fl c
above it is refused at startup rather than discovered thousands of sockets in.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Slowloris against a local server, with a report:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
slowhttptest-ng -u http://127.0.0.1:8080/ -c 200 -l 120 -g -o report
.Ed
.Pp
Find the connection count at which service stops:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
slowhttptest-ng -u http://127.0.0.1:8080/ -c 512 --capacity \e
    --capacity-start 32 --capacity-step 32
.Ed
.Pp
An authenticated JSON endpoint, silently, gated for CI:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
slowhttptest-ng -B -u https://api.example/v1/orders \e
    -1 "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -f application/json -P @order.json \e
    -q -g -o report --fail-on-status 5xx
jq -e '.criterion.pass' report.json
.Ed
.Pp
Attack directly, but measure availability from another network path:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
slowhttptest-ng -u http://target.example/ -e probe-proxy.local:3128 -c 200
.Ed
.Sh COMPATIBILITY
.Nm
accepts every flag
.Xr slowhttptest 1
does, with the same meanings.
It differs in three ways that matter to existing
scripts:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Fl o
writes
.Pa base.html
and
.Pa base.json .
The classic tool wrote
.Pa base.html
and
.Pa base.csv ;
CSV output has been dropped.
.It
Errors exit 2 or 3 rather than the classic's -1.
.It
The report is built around service availability rather than socket state
changes, and states what it could not rule out.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr curl 1 ,
.Xr slowhttptest 1
.Pp
.Lk https://github.com/shekyan/slowhttptest
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Sergey Shekyan
and contributors.
.Sh CAVEATS
A result from a single run, from a single vantage point, cannot distinguish
server-side exhaustion from packet loss, from rate limiting or a WAF shedding
this client specifically, or from an intermediary failing rather than the
origin.
Every report states this alongside its verdict.
